    [1] => Array ( [name] => Dave  [email] => dave@EXAMPLE.COM )
)
```

### Notes

- **`\z` vs `$`** — `\z` means "absolute end of string", so `eve@example.com.evil.net` is correctly rejected (with `$` it would also be rejected here, but `\z` is stricter and immune to a trailing newline).
- **`/i` flag** — email domains are case-insensitive, so `Dave@EXAMPLE.COM` matches.
- **`preg_quote`** — makes the domain safe to pass in as a parameter (e.g. `example.com` → `example\.com`).
- **`array_values`** — drops the original keys so the result is a clean list. Remove it if you want to preserve the original indexes.
- **Missing/empty `email`** — handled with `?? ''` and the empty check, so no PHP warnings are emitted.

